---
import Document from "@/layouts/Document.astro";
import { LAB, OPERATOR, REPO } from "@/lib/site";

const products = [
  {
    name: "MCP auth atlas",
    href: "/mcp/",
    status: "Live",
    text: "Public MCP servers against every requirement of the MCP Authorization specification, one cited observation per cell.",
  },
  {
    name: "Agent auth atlas",
    href: "/standards/",
    status: "Live",
    text: "The specifications, drafts and EU acts behind agent authorization as one map of what builds on and competes with what.",
  },
  {
    name: "Scanner",
    href: "/mcp/scanner/",
    status: "Live",
    text: "A nightly job that reads listed servers' public metadata and proposes new observations as pull requests.",
  },
  {
    name: "Delegation chain explorer",
    href: "/explorer/",
    status: "Live",
    text: "Step-through scenarios with the decoded token at each hop, and toggles for the attacks and misconfigurations that break them.",
  },
  {
    name: "Breach anatomy",
    href: "/breaches/",
    status: "Live",
    text: "Published agent-auth vulnerabilities and incidents, each traced to the missing control, the atlas rows that name it and a replay in the explorer.",
  },
  {
    name: "Agents under EU rules",
    status: "Planned",
    text: "What eIDAS 2.0 and the EUDI wallet, the AI Act and NIS2 ask of agent delegation, as scenarios and long reads.",
  },
];
---

<Document
  title="About"
  heading={LAB}
  description={`${LAB} is an independent, vendor-neutral lab for authentication and authorization in the agent era. letsauth.eu is where its work is published.`}
  path="/about/"
  eyebrow="About"
  lede="An independent, vendor-neutral lab for how AI agents are authenticated and authorized. We measure what the protocols require and what real systems do, and publish all of it as open data."
  updated="2026-10-14"
>
  <h2>Why a lab</h2>
  <p>
    Agents now call APIs, use tools and act for people. The rules for how they get and pass on
    authority sit where agent protocols (MCP, A2A) meet identity standards (OAuth, OpenID
    Connect, token exchange, workload identity), and both sides move monthly. Most of what is
    written about it is assertion. {LAB} replaces assertion with evidence: every claim cites a
    specification revision, every status cites a dated observation, and the data is yours to
    download and check.
  </p>

  <h2>What we build</h2>
  <ul class="products" role="list">
    {
      products.map((p) => (
        <li class="card product">
          <p class="product__head">
            {p.href ? <a href={p.href}>{p.name}</a> : <span>{p.name}</span>}
            <span class:list={["pill", { accent: p.status === "Live" }]}>{p.status}</span>
          </p>
          <p class="product__text">{p.text}</p>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <p>
    All of them are views on one shared data model. The order and status of each is in the{" "}
    <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/roadmap.md`} rel="noopener">roadmap</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How we work</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cited, not asserted.</strong> A claim without a specification revision and section does not ship.</li>
    <li><strong>Neutral.</strong> Nothing is ranked or scored. Rows are alphabetical, and no vendor pays for anything.</li>
    <li><strong>Unknown is honest.</strong> A missing observation reads <em>unknown</em>, never failure.</li>
    <li><strong>Passive by default.</strong> We read public metadata only, on a schedule, never on a visitor's request, and never with credentials.</li>
    <li><strong>Open.</strong> Code under MIT, data under CC BY 4.0, history kept in a public repository where anyone can propose a correction.</li>
    <li><strong>European by default.</strong> Built in Berlin, hosted and measured in the EU; see <a href="/made-in-europe/">made in Europe</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who is behind it</h2>
  <p>
    {LAB} is run by <a href={OPERATOR.site} rel="noopener">{OPERATOR.name}</a>, a security
    engineer in Berlin who works on usable security and web authentication. It is a personal,
    independent project; the <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a> says what that means for
    affiliation.
  </p>

  <h2>Get involved</h2>
  <p>
    Know how a server behaves? <a href="/contribute/">Add an observation</a>. Found a
    mistake, or want a specification on the map? Open an issue in the{" "}
    <a href={REPO} rel="noopener">repository</a>. Anything else:{" "}
    <a href={`mailto:${OPERATOR.email}`}>{OPERATOR.email}</a>.
  </p>
</Document>

<style>
  .products {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(16rem, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-3);
    padding: 0;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-5);
    list-style: none;
  }
  .products .product {
    margin: 0;
    padding: var(--space-4);
  }
  .product__head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: var(--text-md);
  }
  .product__head a {
    color: var(--ink);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .product__head a:hover {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .product__text {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }
</style>
